2025-05-01 03:14:22 - Thread-1261 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables and columns for this task  
• trees  
  – zipcode : links tree records to a postal code in the income file  
  – boroname : borough name we need to aggregate by  
• income_trees  
  – zipcode : join key that matches trees.zipcode  
  – Estimate_Median_income : used in the “> 0” filter  
  – Estimate_Mean_income : averaged per borough and also required “> 0”

Filters / conditions likely used  
• income_trees.Estimate_Median_income > 0  
• income_trees.Estimate_Mean_income > 0  
• trees.boroname IS NOT NULL AND trees.boroname <> ''  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows if no LIMIT already present).  
Each query is preceded by a brief comment explaining its purpose.

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the distinct borough names in the trees table
SELECT DISTINCT boroname
FROM trees
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Get a feel for zipcodes and borough names together
SELECT zipcode, boroname
FROM trees
WHERE boroname IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the income table structure & a few rows
SELECT *
FROM income_trees
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. How many tree rows actually match a zipcode existing in income_trees?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS matched_tree_rows
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode;
```

```sql
-- 5. Examine joined data after applying the income >0 filters
SELECT t.boroname,
       t.zipcode,
       i.Estimate_Median_income,
       i.Estimate_Mean_income
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Count trees per borough that satisfy all conditions
SELECT t.boroname,
       COUNT(*) AS tree_count
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY tree_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Average mean income per borough along with the tree count
SELECT t.boroname,
       COUNT(*)                               AS tree_count,
       AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income)            AS avg_mean_income
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY tree_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. The same as #7 but only keep the top‑3 boroughs by tree count
WITH borough_stats AS (
  SELECT t.boroname,
         COUNT(*)                    AS tree_count,
         AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income) AS avg_mean_income
  FROM trees AS t
  JOIN income_trees AS i
    ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
  WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
    AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
    AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY t.boroname
)
SELECT *
FROM borough_stats
ORDER BY tree_count DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

```sql
-- 9. Show the zips that contribute to the winning boroughs (for sanity check)
WITH top3 AS (
  SELECT t.boroname
  FROM trees AS t
  JOIN income_trees AS i
    ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
  WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
    AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
    AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY t.boroname
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 3
)
SELECT t.boroname,
       t.zipcode,
       COUNT(*) AS trees_here
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
JOIN top3
  ON top3.boroname = t.boroname
GROUP BY t.boroname, t.zipcode
ORDER BY t.boroname, trees_here DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: borough, number of trees, and average mean income
SELECT t.boroname                           AS borough,
       COUNT(*)                             AS num_trees,
       ROUND(AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income),2) AS avg_mean_income
FROM trees AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income  > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY num_trees DESC
LIMIT 3;
```
